     answer="Extra Medium is a marketing agency in Nashville, and social is where this city shows itself off: Broadway nights, writers rounds, new restaurants in East Nashville. We run social media, creators and content as one program, planned by senior people and measured against customers, not followers. No posting just to post.",
     sections=[["The storefront for a city that posts itself", "<p>Nashville may be the most posted city in the South. The visitor economy effectively lives on Instagram and TikTok: trips get planned from saved posts, bachelorette itineraries get built from reels, and a venue&rsquo;s feed is its first impression long before Broadway. Locals run on the same feeds, deciding which East Nashville opening to try and which Nations brewery gets Friday. For a Nashville business, social is not a channel on the list. It is the storefront, and it has to look like the city sounds.</p>"], ["Creators are the local media now", "<p>The country music industry learned this first: the audience trusts voices, not logos. The same is true for a restaurant group, a clinic or a home-services brand. Nashville&rsquo;s creators carry neighborhood-level trust that no ad buy rents, and our background includes influencer marketing run at scale for large brands, applied here at local weight. Our <a href=\"/influencer-marketing-for-local-businesses/\">influencer marketing for local businesses</a> page lays out the model: the right local voices, honest posts, measured like media instead of favors.</p>"], ["A program, not a posting chore", "<p>Most business social fails as a chore: an intern, a stock photo, a caption written in the parking lot. We run it as a program, with a calendar tied to the city&rsquo;s own calendar, festival season, album cycles, tourist peaks and neighborhood moments, planned by the same senior team that owns your wider strategy. When the social plan needs a leader above it, a <a href=\"/fractional-cmo-nashville/\">fractional CMO in Nashville</a> can own the whole function. The measure is customers and bookings, never follower counts reported like results.</p>"]],
     faqs=[["Which platforms do you run for Nashville businesses?", "Instagram and TikTok lead for most consumer brands here, with Facebook still strong for local services and LinkedIn where the buyer is a business. We choose by where your customer actually is, not by habit. A Broadway venue and a B2B firm near the healthcare campuses do not get the same mix."], ["Do you work with Nashville creators and influencers?", "Yes. Creator partnerships are built into the program, from neighborhood voices with a few thousand loyal followers to larger Music City names. The fit test is local trust, not follower count, and every partnership is disclosed and measured like the media it is."], ["How often should a Nashville business post?", "As often as there is something worth saying, on a rhythm your audience can count on. In a city with this much going on, the material is rarely the problem; the planning is. Consistency tied to the city's calendar beats daily filler every time."]],
